{% extends 'base.html.twig' %}
{% block title %}About the technology · MemorialAtlas{% endblock %}
{% block body %}
<article style="max-width:820px">
<span class="eyebrow">About the technology</span>
<h1>Read. Group. Interpret.</h1>
<p>MemorialAtlas is a focused project for the <a href="https://symfony.com/hackathon/">Symfony AI &amp; MCP Hackathon</a>. The technical challenge starts with an ordered photostream: one marker may appear in several photographs, and one photograph may contain several names. OCR alone produces fragments. We need to associate those fragments with physical markers before turning them into useful records.</p>
<figure style="margin:28px 0"><img src="/images/tacelosky-2026-11-14.jpg" width="1600" height="737" alt="Tac Tacelosky in a Symfony wool hat, smiling, with rows of headstones on a grassy slope behind him" style="width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:5px"><figcaption class="muted" style="font-size:.8rem;margin-top:8px">Tac Tacelosky, Symfony super-fan, photographing headstones, 14 November 2026.</figcaption></figure>
<h2>1. OCR each photograph</h2>
<p>We preserve the original image, read its inscription with OCR, and retain available capture metadata, including latitude and longitude. Hosted Mistral OCR and a separate Mistral-compatible RustO endpoint are independently configurable. OCR text stays attached to its source photograph.</p>
<h2>2. Does the next photo belong to the same marker?</h2>
<p>Jev compares consecutive photographs using OCR text, capture order, available latitude and longitude, and any recorded visual descriptions. Matching names and dates can connect a close-up to the previous photograph. Location provides supporting context: nearby GPS coordinates alone cannot distinguish adjacent graves, and camera positions can drift.</p>
<p>The comparison proposes whether two photographs show the same physical marker, different markers, or an uncertain relationship. These are review suggestions; the current capture-review command does not automatically merge records. Different people may share a marker, and the same person may have both a headstone and a footstone.</p>
<h2>3. Combine the text and extract meaning</h2>
<p>Once photographs are grouped into a marker record, structured extraction uses their combined OCR text and available observations to propose names, birth and death dates, inscriptions, and relationship clues. It preserves ambiguities rather than filling gaps with guesses. An explicit marriage inscription is evidence of a relationship; simply appearing together is not enough.</p>
<p>Saved claims retain available provider and model provenance. The record page presents the photographs, OCR, descriptions and AI assessment history together. Extracted names are not automatically resolved person identities; cemetery context helps constrain later genealogy and name resolution.</p>
<h2>Optional image analysis: accuracy and cost</h2>
<p>The baseline workflow can run on OCR and text alone. Optional image analysis examines the photographs themselves, adding evidence about layout, marker shape and visual details. This can substantially improve difficult readings and grouping decisions, but requires additional model calls, processing time and cost.</p>
<p>Uniform markers may gain little from that extra step. Varied, worn or unusually arranged markers can benefit much more. Vision can be disabled for a text-only run, and the chosen processing policy is recorded.</p>
<h2>The stack and the data</h2>
<p>Symfony and Doctrine manage records and processing workflows. Elasticsearch indexes the combined evidence for search. API Platform exposes a public, read-only <a href="/api">API</a> with <a href="/api/docs">interactive documentation</a>, so genealogy projects can reuse the data while retaining source identifiers. FollowTheMoney-based genealogy and other open-source family-tree tools are potential consumers.</p>
<p>The <a href="https://huggingface.co/datasets/museado/memorialatlas-demo">public Sperryville demo on Hugging Face</a> contains a photograph and its saved OCR and extraction results. Developers can load it without our S3 credentials or paid AI calls. It is a small example, not the full cemetery collection.</p>
<h2 id="drone">The drone flight</h2>
<p>On 13 November 2026 we photographed the St. Peter's churchyard twice: close-up phone photographs of each marker, and eight 360° spheres from an Antigravity A1 drone, four in the air and four from the ground. The phone photographs are the MemorialAtlas collection. The spheres are on the <a href="/drone">Drone page</a> for context; they are not part of the hackathon entry.</p>
<style>.drone-pair{display:grid;grid-template-columns:minmax(0,320px) minmax(0,1fr);gap:20px;margin:28px 0;align-items:stretch}.drone-pair figure{margin:0;display:flex;flex-direction:column}.drone-pair img{width:100%;height:auto;border-radius:5px}#drone-map{flex:1;min-height:420px;border-radius:5px}.drone-pair figcaption{font-size:.8rem;margin-top:8px}@media(max-width:700px){.drone-pair{grid-template-columns:1fr}#drone-map{min-height:340px}}</style>
<div class="drone-pair">
<figure><img src="/images/drone-goggles-2026-11-13.jpg" width="676" height="1200" alt="Tac Tacelosky in a tan vest, wearing white drone goggles and holding the motion controller, beside a parked car with wooded hills behind"><figcaption class="muted">Tac Tacelosky flying the A1 through its goggles, 12:58, 13 November 2026.</figcaption></figure>
<figure><div id="drone-map" role="img" aria-label="Satellite view of St. Peter's churchyard with the eight sphere positions"></div><figcaption class="muted">Overhead view. Blue: aerial spheres. Orange: ground spheres. Red: where the goggles photo was taken. Sphere positions are estimates. Click a numbered dot to open that sphere.</figcaption></figure>
</div>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/leaflet/1.9.4/leaflet.min.css">
<script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/leaflet/1.9.4/leaflet.min.js"></script>
<script>
(() => {
  // Same positions as public/drone/st-peter.html; the A1 writes no GPS, so these are estimates.
  const spheres = [
    ['12:50:48', 38.6936045, -78.1689035, 'aerial'], ['12:51:56', 38.6934787, -78.1690646, 'aerial'],
    ['12:52:16', 38.6933529, -78.1689035, 'aerial'], ['12:53:04', 38.6934787, -78.1687424, 'aerial'],
    ['12:58:40', 38.6934981, -78.1689351, 'ground'], ['12:59:30', 38.6934219, -78.1689351, 'ground'],
    ['12:59:45', 38.6934219, -78.1688375, 'ground'], ['13:00:01', 38.6934981, -78.1688375, 'ground'],
  ];
  const pilot = [38.693461, -78.168886];
  const map = L.map('drone-map', { maxZoom: 21, scrollWheelZoom: false });
  L.tileLayer('https://server.arcgisonline.com/ArcGIS/rest/services/World_Imagery/MapServer/tile/{z}/{y}/{x}', { maxNativeZoom: 19, maxZoom: 21, attribution: 'Imagery: Esri, Maxar, Earthstar Geographics' }).addTo(map);
  L.circleMarker(pilot, { radius: 7, color: '#fff', weight: 2, fillColor: '#dc2626', fillOpacity: 1 }).bindTooltip('Goggles photo, 12:58:15 (phone GPS)').addTo(map);
  spheres.forEach(([t, lat, lon, kind], i) => {
    const color = kind === 'aerial' ? '#3b82f6' : '#f59e0b';
    L.marker([lat, lon], { title: `${i + 1}. ${t} ${kind}`, icon: L.divIcon({ className: '', iconSize: [22, 22], iconAnchor: [11, 11],
      html: `<div style="width:22px;height:22px;box-sizing:border-box;border-radius:50%;background:${color};border:2px solid #fff;box-shadow:0 1px 4px #0009;color:#fff;font:600 11px/18px system-ui;text-align:center">${i + 1}</div>` }) })
      .bindTooltip(`${i + 1}. ${t} (${kind})`).on('click', () => { location.href = `/drone#${i + 1}`; }).addTo(map);
  });
  map.fitBounds(L.latLngBounds([pilot, ...spheres.map(s => [s[1], s[2]])]).pad(0.6), { maxZoom: 20 });
})();
</script>
<p>MemorialAtlas works from photographs close enough to read an inscription. From the air the markers are a few pixels across, so the spheres add nothing to OCR, grouping or extraction.</p>
<p>The A1 writes no GPS into its photographs or videos. The flight logs we copied from the visor are encrypted, so we have not read the flight path from them yet. Until we do, each sphere is placed at the nearest phone photograph taken around the same time, and its position on the map is an estimate.</p>
<p>The viewer shows the camera's raw dual-fisheye images without stitching. The aerial spheres look right; the ground spheres show a seam where the two lenses meet. Proper stitching needs the manufacturer's desktop software.</p>
<p>A sphere shows how the markers sit in relation to each other, the church and the road, which close-ups cannot. With positions fixed and stitched images, the spheres could link to the marker records they show.</p>
